[Event "Strategy: the pawn break, pieces first"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/tkBvne6l"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Spielmann - Carls, Baden-Baden 1925 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "2rr2k1/ppqnbpnp/2p1p1p1/8/2PP2N1/1P3BPP/PB2QP2/R4RK1 w - - 6 20"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2rr2k1/ppqnbpnp/2p1p1p1/8/2PP2N1/1P3BPP/PB2QP2/R4RK1_w_-_-_6_20"]

{ A pawn can never step back, so every push needs a plan. Five plans, starting now. The pawn break: a pawn push that hits his pawn to open lines for your pieces. Play it when they already stand behind it. You have White: rook to d1 first. [%cal Ya1d1] }
20. Rad1 { Your rook comes behind your d pawn on the d column, and your dark-squared bishop already aims through d4 on the long diagonal. If his queen later raids c2, the rook chases her. [%cal Gd1d4,Gb2d4] } (20. d5 { The break at once, with no rook behind your d pawn yet. } 20... cxd5 21. cxd5 Qc2 22. Rfe1 Qxe2 23. Rxe2 { Slightly better for you, no more: with no rook on d1, his queen raided c2 and traded herself off, and nothing of his fell. [%cal Ga1d1] }) (20. Rfd1 { The f1 rook goes to d1 instead. About as good by the numbers, but now your other rook cannot get past it to the e column. } 20... Nf5 21. d5 cxd5 22. cxd5 Qd6 { Better for you, but no pawn falls: his queen blocks your d pawn from d6, and your a1 rook never reached the e column. [%csl Rd6,Ge1] }) (20. Nh6+ { The knight checks from h6. About as good by the numbers, but it is a knight trip, not a step towards the break. } 20... Kf8 21. h4 Nf6 { Better for you, but your knight sits out on h6, and your d pawn still has no rook behind it. [%cal Ga1d1] }) (20. Rfe1 { The rook goes to e1, on the column of his e6 pawn, not behind your d pawn. } 20... h5 21. Nh6+ Kf8 22. Qe3 Nf6 23. Rad1 { Slightly better for you: his h pawn made your knight jump to h6, and your d pawn got its rook only now. [%csl Rh6] }) (20. Rae1 { The a1 rook goes to the e column instead of behind your d pawn. } 20... h5 21. Nh6+ Kf8 22. Qd2 b5 { Slightly better for you: no rook stands behind your d pawn, and his b pawn now hits your pawn on c4. [%csl Gd1][%cal Rb5c4] }) 20... Nf5 { His knight hops to f5. Your rook and your dark-squared bishop both stand behind your d pawn now: push it to d5! [%cal Gd1d4,Gb2d4,Yd4d5] } 21. d5 { That is the break: your pawn hits both his pawns in front of it, and your bishop on b2 now sees all the way down the long diagonal. [%cal Gb2h8,Gd5c6,Gd5e6] } (21. Ne3 { The knight steps back to e3: safe, and slow. } 21... Nxe3 22. Qxe3 Bf6 { About level: while your knight stepped back, his bishop came to f6, right on your bishop's long diagonal, and the break lost its bite. [%cal Gd4d5,Rb2f6] }) (21. Kg2 { The king steps to g2, a quiet move before the break. } 21... Re8 22. d5 cxd5 23. cxd5 e5 { Slightly better for you, no more: his rook got to e8 in time. If your knight takes on e5, his bishop steps aside and his rook pins the knight to your queen. [%csl Ge5][%cal Re8e2] }) (21. Be4 { The bishop hits his knight on f5. About as good by the numbers, but it only chases a piece. } 21... Nf6 22. Bb1 c5 23. d5 Nxg4 24. hxg4 { Better for you, yet it was a detour: the break came two moves later, and his e pawn has not fallen. [%csl Ge6] }) (21. Rd3 { The rook lifts to d3 before the break. Too slow: he gets a free move. } 21... h5 22. Ne3 Nxe3 23. Rxe3 Bf6 { About level: his h pawn chased your knight, a pair of knights came off, and his bishop now stands on f6 in your bishop's way. [%cal Gd4d5,Rb2f6] }) 21... cxd5 { He takes on d5. Take back with the pawn. [%cal Yc4d5] } 22. cxd5 { Your c pawn takes back, and the new pawn on d5 hits e6 in turn. The long diagonal stays open. [%cal Gd5e6,Gb2h8] } 22... e5 { His e pawn jumps to e5 to close the long diagonal. Take it with the knight! [%cal Yg4e5] } 23. Nxe5 { Your knight takes the pawn, and the bishop on b2 waits behind it on the long diagonal. [%cal Gb2e5] } (23. Be4 { The bishop hits his knight on f5 instead. Nearly as good by the numbers, but it leaves the pawn standing. } 23... Nd6 24. Rc1 Qb8 25. Rxc8 Qxc8 { Better for you, but the pawn on e5 still stands: his knight went to d6, and a pair of rooks came off. [%cal Gg4e5] }) (23. Bxe5 { The bishop takes first. It looks the same, but it spends the piece that should take last. } 23... Nxe5 24. Qxe5 h5 25. Qxc7 Rxc7 { Level, though a pawn up: the queens came off, and his h pawn now hits your knight on g4. [%cal Gg4e5] }) 23... Nxe5 { His knight recaptures. Now the piece that waited: bishop takes on e5! [%cal Yb2e5] } 24. Bxe5 { That is the pawn break: the bishop that waited on the long diagonal took the knight that took back. You are a pawn up, your bishop on e5 hits his queen, and your d pawn is passed. [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge5c7] } (24. Qxe5 { The queen takes back instead of the bishop, and she stands where his queen can meet her. } 24... Qxe5 25. Bxe5 f6 26. Ba1 Kf7 { Better for you and a pawn up, but he traded queens, and his f pawn chased your bishop back to a1 and blocks the long diagonal. [%cal Ra1f6] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the pawn break, Bled 1931"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/WPJq7pmh"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Stoltz - Alekhine, Bled 1931 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "1r2r1k1/2p1bppp/p1qp1n2/8/1p2P3/3Q4/PPPBNPPP/R4RK1 w - - 2 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1r2r1k1/2p1bppp/p1qp1n2/8/1p2P3/3Q4/PPPBNPPP/R4RK1_w_-_-_2_17"]

{ You have Black, at Bled in 1931. }
17. f3 { His f pawn steps up one square. It looks solid, but it is a mistake. What now? } 17... d5 { The pawn break: your d pawn hits his pawn on e4, and your pieces stand ready. } (17... Rbd8 { A quiet rook move to d8. Your pieces were ready for the break already, so it only hands him a free move. } 18. Kh1 d5 19. e5 Nd7 20. Nd4 Qb6 { About level: his king slipped into the corner first, so your break came with no check to follow, and his pawn on e5 still stands. [%csl Ge5] }) (17... Nd7 { The knight steps back to d7. Quiet, and he uses the time. } 18. Nd4 Qb6 19. Be3 { About level: his knight came to d4 and his bishop to e3, both guarding the centre. [%cal Gd6d5] }) (17... Bf8 { The bishop drops back to f8, and he gets the time to bring a knight over. } 18. Nf4 Nd7 19. Be1 Ne5 20. Qd2 { Level: his knight on f4 guards the centre, and your break has not come. [%cal Gd6d5] }) (17... a5 { The a pawn marches on the queen's side. It is slow, and it touches none of his pawns yet. } 18. c4 a4 19. b3 Nd7 20. h3 { Level: his pawn on b3 now blocks your b pawn, and your break never came. [%cal Gd6d5] }) (17... Qc5+ { Check at once, with the queen: too early, before your break. } 18. Be3 Qb5 19. Nd4 Qb7 20. Rad1 { Level: his bishop blocked the check with a hit on your queen, she ran back to b7, and his pieces now guard the centre. [%cal Gd6d5] }) (17... Qb6+ { The queen checks from b6 at once, before your break. } 18. Be3 Qb5 19. Nd4 { Level: his bishop blocked on e3 with a hit on your queen, and his knight came to d4. [%cal Gd6d5] }) (17... b3 { A break on the queen's side: your b pawn hits his pawns there, with your rook behind it. It leaves the centre alone. } 18. Nd4 Qb6 19. axb3 { Slightly better for him: his a pawn took your b pawn, and you are simply a pawn down. [%cal Gd6d5] }) 18. e5 { His pawn steps past yours to e5 and hits your knight on f6. What now? [%cal Re5f6] } 18... Bc5+ { Check! The bishop leaves through d6, the square your pawn left, and by stepping off the e column it opens it for your rook. [%cal Gc5g1,Ge8e5] } (18... Qc5+ { Check with the queen, but your bishop stays on e7, so your rook on e8 still sees only your bishop. } 19. Be3 Qc4 20. Qxc4 dxc4 21. exf6 Bxf6 { Much better for him: his bishop blocked with a hit on your queen, the queens came off, and your knight was lost for a pawn. [%csl Re3] }) (18... Qb6+ { The queen checks from b6, and your bishop still stands in front of your rook. } 19. Kh1 Nd7 20. f4 Nc5 21. Qf3 { Level: your knight got out, but his pawn on e5 now has the f pawn behind it, and your rook still faces your bishop. [%csl Ge5][%cal Rf4e5] }) (18... Nd7 { The knight steps back to d7, out of danger. Safe, but his e5 pawn stays. } 19. f4 Nc5 20. Qh3 Na4 21. Be3 { Slightly better for you, but his pawn on e5 is guarded by the f pawn now, and your bishop never came out with check. [%cal Ge7c5] }) (18... Qc4 { Your queen goes to c4 and offers a trade. } 19. Be3 Bf8 20. f4 Ng4 21. Bd4 Qxd3 22. cxd3 { About level: his pawn never took your knight, since that would have opened the e column for your rook. Now his f pawn guards e5, and the queens are off. [%csl Ge5][%cal Rf4e5] }) (18... Ne4 { The knight jumps to e4, into the middle of his pawns. } 19. fxe4 dxe4 20. Qb3 { Much better for him: his f pawn took your knight, and one pawn back is nothing like enough. [%cal Ge7c5] }) (18... Ng4 { The knight jumps to g4 and offers itself. } 19. fxg4 Qb5 20. Qf3 f6 21. Nd4 { Lost for you: his f pawn took the knight, and you got nothing for it. [%cal Ge7c5] }) (18... Qe6 { Your queen steps across to e6 to guard the knight, but a guard does not stop a pawn. } 19. exf6 Bc5+ 20. Kh1 Qxe2 21. Qxd5 Qe5 22. Qxe5 Rxe5 { Level: his pawn took your knight, your queen took his knight, and his queen took your d pawn. His pawn on f6 is loose. [%cal Gg7f6] }) 19. Kh1 { His king steps into the corner, and your knight is still under attack. What now? } 19... Rxe5 { That is the pawn break: the rook stood ready on e8, and the break let your bishop out with check. You win a pawn. His slip was that f pawn push at the start: the knight to g3 was better. Alekhine, world champion from 1927, had Black. [%csl Ge5] } (19... Qb5 { Your queen steps to b5 and offers a trade, leaving his e5 pawn alone. } 20. Qxb5 Rxb5 21. c3 Rxe5 22. Nd4 Bxd4 23. cxd4 { Slightly better for you: had his pawn taken your knight, your rook would take his on e2. You won his e pawn, but the queens came off. [%csl Ge5] }) (19... Nd7 { The knight steps back to d7 instead of your rook taking. } 20. f4 { Slightly better for you: his f pawn came to f4 in time, and the pawn on e5 is safe. [%csl Ge5][%cal Rf4e5] }) (19... Ne4 { Your knight hops to e4 to dodge his pawn. } 20. fxe4 Rxe5 21. exd5 Rxd5 22. Qf3 { Much better for him: you gave a knight and got a single pawn back, and his queen now hits your rook. [%cal Rf3d5] }) (19... Qe6 { Your queen steps to e6 to guard the knight and hit e5, instead of taking the pawn at once. } 20. exf6 Qxe2 21. Qxd5 Qe5 22. Qxe5 Rxe5 { Level: his pawn took your knight anyway. Your queen took his knight in return, but your d pawn fell, and his pawn on f6 has no guard. [%cal Gg7f6] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the pawn chain, Lasker at New York 1924"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/DUo3mxTF"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Alekhine - Lasker, New York 1924 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "2r1rnk1/p1b2bpp/2pq1p2/3p1P2/3P2P1/3B1N2/PQ3PNP/R3R1K1 b - - 4 24"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r1rnk1/p1b2bpp/2pq1p2/3p1P2/3P2P1/3B1N2/PQ3PNP/R3R1K1_b_-_-_4_24"]

{ You have Black, as Emanuel Lasker did, world champion for twenty-seven years. His pawns on g4 and f5 stand corner to corner, the back one guarding the front: that is a pawn chain. Attack it at its back pawn, the base, with your h pawn. [%csl Rg4][%cal Yh7h5,Rg4f5] }
24... h5 { Your h pawn marches straight at g4, the base of his chain, and nothing of his guards it. [%csl Rg4][%cal Gh5g4] } (24... g6 { Your g pawn hits f5, the front of his chain. But g4 still guards it, and the base stands untouched. [%csl Rg4][%cal Rg4f5] } 25. h4 Rxe1+ 26. Rxe1 { Level: his chain still stands, and not one pawn of his fell. Hitting the front got you nothing. [%cal Gh7h5] }) (24... Rxe1+ { You trade a pair of rooks. Fair enough, but it leaves his chain alone, with its base on g4. [%csl Rg4] } 25. Rxe1 Rb8 26. Qc1 { Slightly better for you, but a pair of rooks came off and no pawn of his chain fell. [%cal Gh7h5] }) (24... h6 { The h pawn takes one step only. From h6 it hits nothing, and his base on g4 is safe. [%csl Rg4] } 25. h4 Nd7 26. Rxe8+ Rxe8 { About level: one pair of rooks traded, his h pawn came up to h4, and his chain never felt a thing. [%csl Rg4] }) (24... Qd7 { Your queen lines up on f5, the front of his chain. But his bishop and his g pawn both guard it. [%cal Gd7f5,Rd3f5,Rg4f5] } 25. Rec1 Qd6 26. Qc3 { Level: your queen went back to d6, a move spent, and his chain stands exactly as before. [%cal Gh7h5] }) (24... Rb8 { The rook goes to b8 and hits his queen, but it leaves his chain alone. [%cal Gb8b2] } 25. Qd2 Nd7 26. Rxe8+ Rxe8 { Slightly better for you, yet g4 was never hit, and his chain is whole. [%cal Gh7h5] }) (24... Nd7 { The knight comes out to d7, a useful move, but his chain gets more time to live. } 25. Rxe8+ Rxe8 26. Qb7 Bb6 { About level: his queen got in to b7, and his chain still stands without a scratch. [%cal Gh7h5] }) (24... Bh5 { Your bishop hits g4 from h5, a piece against the base. But his g pawn simply takes it. [%cal Rg4h5] } 25. gxh5 { Lost for you: his g pawn took a whole bishop, and you got nothing for it. [%csl Rh5] }) 25. g5 { His g pawn runs on to g5, and the chain is broken: his f pawn has no pawn behind it now. Only his knight guards g5: take it with your f pawn. [%cal Yf6g5,Rf3g5] } 25... fxg5 { A pawn up: that is how you attack a pawn chain, at its base. The base pawn fled to g5, straight into your f pawn. If his knight takes back, your queen, backed by the bishop, crashes into h2 with check and mates. [%csl Gg5][%cal Gc7h2] } (25... Rxe1+ { You trade rooks before taking on g5, and that lets his last rook come to e1, on the open e column. } 26. Rxe1 fxg5 27. h3 { Slightly better for you: the pawn is yours, but his rook now holds the open e column, and your edge has shrunk. [%cal Re1e8][%csl Gg5] }) (25... Rb8 { Your rook heads for b8 first, leaving the pawn on g5 for later. } 26. Qd2 fxg5 27. Qxg5 { About level: you took on g5 a move late, and his queen was there to take back. [%csl Rg5] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the pawn chain, Montreal 1923"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/WM4N3DQh"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Alekhine - Paquin, Montreal 1923, simultaneous display (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "rn1q1rk1/pp3ppp/4bn2/3pN1B1/2pP4/2P2B2/P1P2PPP/R2QR1K1 b - - 0 12"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rn1q1rk1/pp3ppp/4bn2/3pN1B1/2pP4/2P2B2/P1P2PPP/R2QR1K1_b_-_-_0_12"]

{ Montreal, 1923: you have White, as Alexander Alekhine did, the future world champion, playing many opponents at once. }
12... b5 { He pushes his b pawn two squares, and that is a mistake: his knight from b8 to d7 was the move. How do you make him pay? } 13. a4 { Your a pawn hits b5, the base your pawn can reach, and nothing of his guards it. [%csl Rb5][%cal Ga4b5] } (13. Rb1 { Your rook comes to b1 and hits b5, a piece against the base. Just as good in the numbers, but a pawn can guard it. [%cal Gb1b5] } 13... a6 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Ng4 f5 { Better for you, but his a pawn props up b5, and no pawn of his chain fell. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (13. Bxf6 { You take the knight and break up his king side, a fine move by the numbers. But it leaves his chain on the queen side alone. } 13... gxf6 14. Ng4 Nc6 { Better for you: his king side pawns are split, but his chain still stands in one piece. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (13. Qc1 { The queen steps to c1, behind your bishop. A decent move, but it leaves his chain alone. [%cal Gc1g5] } 13... h6 14. Bh4 g5 15. Bg3 Nbd7 { Slightly better for you: his pawns chased your bishop back to g3, and you let his chain be: not one pawn of it came off. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (13. Qd2 { The queen steps up to d2, and b5 is left for later. } 13... h6 14. Bxf6 Qxf6 15. Rab1 Nd7 16. Rxb5 Nxe5 17. dxe5 { Better for you: the rook won b5 two moves later, and he used the time to trade off your strong knight. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (13. Re3 { Your rook lifts to e3, ready to swing across to his king. But he gets a free move to bring his knight out. [%cal Be3g3] } 13... Nbd7 14. Nxd7 Qxd7 { About level: his queen now guards b5, and the base you could hit is covered. [%cal Rd7b5,Ga2a4] }) (13. Re2 { The rook steps up to e2, a quiet move, and b5 is left alone for now. } 13... Nbd7 14. Qd2 Qb6 { Slightly better for you, but his queen on b6 now guards b5, and his chain holds. [%cal Rb6b5,Ga2a4] }) (13. Nxf7 { Your knight grabs his f pawn, right next to his king. But his bishop takes the knight. } 13... Bxf7 { Much better for him: you gave a whole knight for one pawn, and his chain still stands. [%cal Ga2a4] }) 13... Nbd7 { His knight comes out and hits yours. What now? } 14. axb5 { A pawn up! You hit his pawn chain at its base, and your a pawn swallowed the base pawn itself on b5. He could not afford to take your a pawn instead: your knight would take his c4 pawn. [%csl Gb5] } (14. Nxd7 { Your knight takes his on d7, but now his queen can take back and guard b5. [%csl Rb5][%cal Rd8d7] } 14... Qxd7 { Slightly better for you, but his queen came to d7 and now guards b5. [%cal Rd7b5,Ga4b5] }) (14. Qd2 { You leave b5 standing and bring the queen to d2. } 14... bxa4 15. Nxd7 Bxd7 16. Bxd5 { Slightly better for you: you won back a pawn on d5, since his knight is pinned to his queen, but his b pawn took your a pawn and got away. [%cal Rg5d8][%csl Ra4] }) (14. Bxf6 { You swap bishop for knight on f6 first, and his knight takes back from d7. } 14... Nxf6 15. axb5 Qb6 16. Qe2 Qxb5 { Slightly better for you, but his queen took the pawn back on b5. [%csl Rb5] }) (14. Qc1 { The queen steps to c1 before you take, and his queen gets a move. } 14... Qa5 15. axb5 Qxb5 16. Nxd7 Nxd7 { Slightly better for you, but his queen came round to take b5 back, and the extra pawn is gone. [%csl Rb5] }) (14. Rb1 { Your rook hits b5 from b1. But his b pawn can take your a pawn and get away. [%cal Gb1b5,Rb5a4] } 14... bxa4 15. Qc1 Qc8 16. h3 a5 { About level: his b pawn took your a pawn and slipped away from b5, and you are a pawn down. [%csl Ra4] }) (14. Re3 { Your rook lifts to e3 and leaves b5 alone, which hands him the move he wanted. } 14... bxa4 { Level, even a pawn down: his b pawn took your a pawn and got away from b5. [%csl Ra4] }) (14. Re2 { The rook steps up to e2, and his b pawn is free to take on a4. } 14... bxa4 { Level, though your a pawn is gone: his b pawn escaped from b5 by taking it. [%csl Ra4] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, block it, then win it"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/gwce3FF3"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Lasker - Tarrasch, St Petersburg 1914 (pgnmentor); Nimzowitsch, My System"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/1bn2n2/8/3p4/1N3NP1/PP2PPBP/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 2 11"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/1bn2n2/8/3p4/1N3NP1/PP2PPBP/R1BQ1RK1_w_kq_-_2_11"]

{ You have White. His pawn on d4 is an isolani, Nimzowitsch's name in his book My System for the isolated pawn: a pawn with no pawn of its own on either column beside it, so no pawn can ever guard it. Blockade it, then win it: queen to d3. [%cal Yd1d3][%csl Rd4] }
11. Qd3 { That is the blockade: your queen stands on the square in front of his isolani, so it cannot step forward, and she hits it too. [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd3d4] } (11. Nfxd4 { The knight grabs the pawn at once, before the blockade, and walks into his pieces. } 11... Nxd4 12. Nxd4 Qxd4 13. Qxd4 Bxd4 { Much better for him: after the trades on d4 you have given a knight for a pawn. [%cal Gd1d3] }) (11. Nbxd4 { The knight from b3 grabs the pawn before the blockade, while his bishop, knight and queen still guard d4. [%cal Rb6d4,Rc6d4,Rd8d4] } 11... Nxd4 12. Nxd4 Qxd4 13. Qxd4 Bxd4 { Much better for him: his bishop sits on d4 when the smoke clears, and you are a piece down for a pawn. [%cal Gd1d3] }) (11. e3 { Level in the numbers, but it throws the plan away: your e pawn hits his pawn, and the trade frees him. } 11... dxe3 12. Qxd8+ Kxd8 13. Bxe3 Bxe3 14. fxe3 { Level: his weak pawn is traded off, the queens are gone, and now your own pawn on e3 is the isolated one. [%cal Gd1d3] }) (11. Bf4 { Level in the numbers: the bishop comes out first, and your queen reaches d3 a move later. } 11... O-O 12. Qd3 g6 13. Rad1 Bf5 14. Qb5 { Level: his bishop reached f5 and chased your queen off d3, the square in front of his pawn. [%csl Gd3] }) (11. a4 { Level in the numbers: the a pawn wants to chase his bishop off b6, but nothing stops his pawn yet. } 11... a5 { Level: his a pawn steps up to meet yours, and your queen is still not on d3. [%cal Gd1d3] }) (11. Qc2 { Level in the numbers: the queen comes out to c2 instead of d3, so nothing stands in front of his pawn. } 11... O-O 12. Rd1 Be6 { Level: your queen never blocked the pawn, and his bishop now hits your knight on b3. [%cal Gd1d3] }) 11... O-O { He castles. Now put your rook behind the queen: rook to d1. [%cal Yf1d1] } 12. Rd1 { The rook lines up behind your queen on the d column. No pawn of his can ever come to the aid of his pawn on d4, so only his pieces can guard it. [%cal Gd1d3,Gd3d4] } (12. Nfxd4 { The knight takes too early: your rook is not behind the queen yet. } 12... Nxd4 13. Nxd4 Qxd4 14. Qxd4 Bxd4 { Much better for him: the trades on d4 leave you a knight down for a pawn. [%cal Gf1d1] }) (12. Nbxd4 { The knight from b3 grabs a move too soon, before the rook has come. } 12... Bxd4 13. Nxd4 Qxd4 14. Qxd4 Nxd4 { Much better for him: his knight sits on d4 after the trades, and you are a piece down for a pawn. [%cal Gf1d1] }) (12. Bf4 { Level in the numbers: the bishop develops, but your rook stays off the d column. } 12... Nd5 13. Bd2 Qf6 { Level: his knight chased your bishop back to d2, and his queen now guards his pawn from f6. [%cal Gf1d1] }) (12. Bg5 { The bishop pins his knight to his queen, but his pawns can chase it. } 12... h6 13. Bf4 g5 14. Be5 { Level: his h and g pawns chased your bishop to e5, and his pawn on d4 still stands. [%cal Gf1d1] }) 12... Qe7 { His queen steps off the d column and no longer guards his pawn. Take it now: the knight from f3 to d4. [%cal Yf3d4] } 13. Nfxd4 { The blockade has done its job: the knight takes his pawn on d4. He can take back, but your other knight and your queen are ready to take again. [%cal Gb3d4,Gd3d4] } (13. Nbxd4 { Just as good: the other knight takes the pawn. } 13... Rd8 14. Be3 { About level, and a pawn up all the same: your bishop guards the knight on d4. [%csl Gd4] }) (13. Bg5 { The bishop pins his knight instead of taking the pawn. } 13... Rd8 14. Rac1 h6 15. Bxf6 Qxf6 { Level: the pin won nothing, and his pawn on d4 still stands, with his rook guarding it now. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (13. Bf4 { The bishop comes out to f4, and the moment to take passes. } 13... Rd8 14. Bg5 h6 15. Bxf6 Qxf6 { About level: his rook came over to guard the pawn, and your bishop was traded off for nothing. [%cal Gf3d4] }) 13... Nxd4 { He takes back on d4. Take again with the knight from b3. } 14. Nxd4 { Your knight takes back: his pawn is gone, and your knight stands on d4, where his pawn stood. } 14... Rd8 { His rook comes to d8 and pins your knight to your queen, and his bishop hits it too. Guard it with a pawn: pawn to e3. [%cal Rd8d4,Rb6d4,Ye2e3] } 15. e3 { The pawn guards your knight, and the pin costs you nothing. His isolani was blocked, then won: you are a pawn up, with your knight on d4, where the pawn stood. [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge3d4] } (15. Bf4 { The bishop develops, but it adds no guard to your pinned knight. } 15... Rxd4 16. Qxd4 Bxd4 17. Rxd4 { Much better for him: the pin cost you the knight, and after the trades he has the only queen. [%cal Ge2e3] }) (15. Bg5 { The bishop hits his knight, but nothing new guards yours on d4. } 15... Rxd4 16. Qxd4 Bxd4 17. Rxd4 { Much better for him: the trades on d4 swapped your queen and knight for his rook and bishop. [%cal Ge2e3] }) (15. Be3 { Just as good in the numbers: your bishop, not a pawn, guards the knight. } 15... Ng4 { Level: still a pawn up, but his knight hits your bishop at once. [%cal Rg4e3] }) (15. Qc3 { Close in the numbers: your queen steps aside to c3 and guards the knight from there. } 15... Be6 16. e3 { Level: a pawn up, with the knight guarded three times now, but your queen had to leave the d column to do it. [%cal Gc3d4,Ge3d4,Gd1d4] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, Moscow 1925"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/CjWcL0rv"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Marshall - Lasker, Moscow 1925 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "r2r2k1/pp3pp1/2p1n2p/3bN3/3PB2q/2Q2R2/PP3PPP/R5K1 w - - 10 22"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2r2k1/pp3pp1/2p1n2p/3bN3/3PB2q/2Q2R2/PP3PPP/R5K1_w_-_-_10_22"]

{ You have Black. His pawn on d4 is an isolani: no pawn of his can ever guard it. }
22. Bxd5 { His bishop takes yours. A mistake: his rook to e3, guarding his bishop, was the move. What now? } 22... Rxd5 { That is the blockade: your rook takes back in front of his isolani, so it cannot step forward, and the rook hits it. [%cal Gd5d4,Ge6d4,Gh4d4] } (22... cxd5 { The pawn takes back instead. Now your own pawn stands in front of his, and your rook on d8 no longer sees his isolani. } 23. Rxf7 Rac8 24. Qg3 Qxg3 25. hxg3 Nxd4 26. Rxb7 { Slightly better for him: you won the pawn, but his rook took two of yours on the seventh row, and you are a pawn down. [%csl Rb7] }) (22... Qxd4 { The queen grabs the pawn at once and leaves his bishop on d5 alive. } 23. Qxd4 Nxd4 24. Bxf7+ Kh7 { Lost for you: his bishop took f7 with check, and you are a whole piece down. [%cal Gd8d5] }) (22... Nxd4 { The knight goes straight for the pawn on d4, and his bishop on d5 is never taken. } 23. Bxf7+ Kh8 24. Ng6+ { Lost for you: his bishop swept up f7 with check, and his knight on g6 now forks your king and queen. [%cal Gd8d5] }) 23. Re1 { He guards his knight on e5 with the rook. What now? } 23... Nxd4 { Blocked, then won: your knight takes the pawn on d4, a pawn up. Your rook and your queen guard it, the queen along the fourth row, and it hits his rook on f3. [%cal Gd5d4,Gh4d4,Rd4f3] } (23... Qxd4 { Your queen takes the pawn herself, but his rook on f3 stays free to guard f7, so his knight can take there. [%cal Rf3f7] } 24. Nxf7 Rf8 25. Ne5 { About level: your queen won the pawn on d4, but his knight took f7, and the pawns are even. [%csl Gd4] }) (23... Rxd4 { The blockading rook takes the pawn itself and leaves d5. } 24. Rxf7 { Better for him: with your rook gone from d5, his knight is free, and his rook took f7. [%csl Rf7] }) (23... Rad8 { The other rook joins on the d column, a natural move, but his knight can take f7 and hit that rook from there. } 24. Nxf7 Nxd4 25. Nxd8 Nxf3+ 26. Qxf3 Qxd8 { Level: his knight jumped to f7 and took your rook on d8, and after the trades the count is even. [%cal Ge6d4] }) (23... Rf8 { Just as good in the numbers: the rook guards f7 first. [%cal Gf8f7] } 24. Qa3 Qxd4 25. Rfe3 { Better for you: your queen collected the pawn on d4 instead, the same pawn by another road. [%csl Gd4] }) (23... Ng5 { The knight hops to g5 to hit his rook, and leaves his pawn alone. } 24. Rd3 { Level: his rook now guards the pawn from d3, and the chance is gone. [%cal Rd3d4] }) (23... c5 { Your c pawn strikes at his isolani, but trading it off only sets him free. } 24. dxc5 Rxc5 { Slightly better for him: the pawns traded on c5, his weak pawn is gone, and so is your target. [%cal Ge6d4] }) (23... Re8 { The rook takes the open e column, but his rook on f3 still guards f7, so his knight can take there. [%cal Rf3f7] } 24. Nxf7 Qxd4 25. Qxd4 Rxd4 { Level: his knight took f7, you took his pawn after all, and with the queens traded the count is even. [%csl Rf7] }) (23... Rdd8 { The rook steps back off d5, and the blockade is gone. } 24. Nxf7 Ng5 25. Nxg5 Qxg5 { Better for him: his knight took f7 the moment your rook stepped back, and you are a pawn down. [%cal Ge6d4] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, a bishop ending"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/GVisBHRS"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Tartakower - Duras, Vienna 1907 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "8/4b3/1p2k1pp/p3Pp2/P4K1P/2P5/1P6/4B3 w - - 2 41"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/4b3/1p2k1pp/p3Pp2/P4K1P/2P5/1P6/4B3_w_-_-_2_41"]

{ You have Black. The bishops run on the same colour, and your king stands on e6. }
41. b4 { He pushes his b pawn to b4, to trade on a5. A mistake. What now? } 41... g5+ { Your g pawn checks his king, the only guard of his isolani on e5, to drive it off f4. [%cal Gg5f4][%csl Re5] } (41... Kd5 { Your king leaves e6 for d5, and nothing blocks his pawn any more. } 42. bxa5 bxa5 43. c4+ Kxc4 44. Bxa5 Bxh4 { About level, though you are a pawn up: his check with the c pawn opened the road for his bishop to a5, and his a pawn is passed. [%cal Gg6g5] }) (41... Kf7 { Your king steps back to f7 and gives up the square in front of his pawn. } 42. Bf2 Bd8 43. h5 gxh5 44. Kxf5 { Level: with your king gone from e6, his king walked in and took f5. [%cal Gg6g5] }) (41... axb4 { You take on b4 first, and the trade frees his a pawn. } 42. cxb4 g5+ 43. Kg3 Kxe5 44. a5 { About level: e5 fell all the same, but his a pawn is free now and stands on a5. [%cal Gg6g5] }) (41... Bd8 { The bishop drops back to d8. Slow: your g pawn still has to drive his king away. } 42. bxa5 g5+ 43. Kf3 bxa5 44. c4 Kxe5 { Much better for you: e5 fell all the same, one move later than it had to, after he traded pawns on a5. [%csl Ge5] }) (41... b5 { Your b pawn pushes to make a passed a pawn: another plan. } 42. axb5 a4 43. c4 a3 44. Bc3 Bxh4 { Much better for you, by that other plan: your a pawn has reached a3, but his isolani on e5 still stands. [%cal Gg6g5] }) (41... Kd7 { The king steps aside to d7, and the blockade on e6 is gone. } 42. Bf2 b5 43. axb5 a4 44. c4 Bxb4 { Level: his bishop reached f2, heading for a square that guards his isolani, and with your king gone from e6 the pawn still stands. [%cal Gg6g5] }) (41... Bxh4 { Your bishop grabs his h pawn, but his bishop is there to take back. } 42. Bxh4 g5+ 43. Bxg5 hxg5+ 44. Kxg5 Kxe5 { Level: the bishops came off, and his bishop and king took your g and h pawns while you took h4 and e5. [%cal Gg6g5] }) (41... Bxb4 { Your bishop takes on b4, a pawn for now. } 42. cxb4 axb4 43. Bxb4 { Lost for you: after the trades on b4 you have one pawn for your bishop. [%cal Gg6g5] }) (41... Bf8 { The bishop goes the long way round, toward g7, to hit e5 from there. } 42. Bf2 { Level: your bishop needs one more move to hit e5, and his bishop on f2 needs one more to guard it. [%cal Gg6g5] }) 42. hxg5 { He takes your g pawn with his h pawn. What now? } 42... hxg5+ { The h pawn takes back with check, and from g5 it keeps his king off f4 for good. [%cal Gg5f4] } (42... Bxg5+ { The bishop takes back with check instead of the pawn. } 43. Kf3 Kxe5 { Level: you still took e5, but your f and h pawns now stand apart, each alone, and the win is gone. [%csl Rf5,Rh6] }) 43. Ke3 { His king has to step back. } 43... Kxe5 { Your king takes his isolani on e5: a pawn up, king in the middle. At the start, his bishop to f2, heading over to guard it, was the move. } (43... Bd8 { The bishop starts for c7 instead of taking at once. } 44. Kd4 { Much better for you, but his king reached d4 and guards e5 now: the pawn that was free is covered. [%cal Ge6e5] }) (43... f4+ { The f pawn checks first, instead of taking the free pawn. } 44. Ke4 { Level: the check let his king step up to e4, and now it guards his pawn. [%cal Ge6e5] }) (43... Bxb4 { Your bishop grabs the pawn on b4, instead of your king taking the one on e5. } 44. cxb4 axb4 45. Kd4 { Level: your bishop went for two pawns, and his king came to d4 to guard his isolani. [%cal Ge6e5] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: hanging pawns, Rubinstein against Nimzowitsch"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/KDOm4MpA"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Rubinstein - Nimzowitsch, Gothenburg 1920 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "r4rk1/pb2nppp/1n3q2/2pp4/5N2/2N3P1/PP1QPPBP/R2R2K1 w - - 10 16"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/pb2nppp/1n3q2/2pp4/5N2/2N3P1/PP1QPPBP/R2R2K1_w_-_-_10_16"]

{ You have White. Hanging pawns: two pawns side by side with no pawn of their own on the columns either side. Strong together, weak once one is forced forward. His c and d pawns have no b or e pawn beside them. Pawn to b3! [%csl Rc5,Rd5][%cal Yb2b3] }
16. b3 { Akiba Rubinstein, one of the best players of the 1920s, played this: if his c pawn runs forward to c4, your b pawn takes it, and the pair is broken. [%cal Gb3c4] } (16. Ncxd5 { The plan done wrong: you grab the pawn at once, and the knight leaving c3 opens the road from his queen to your b pawn. [%cal Rf6b2] } 16... Bxd5 17. Nxd5 Nexd5 18. Bxd5 Nxd5 19. Qxd5 Qxb2 { Level: you won d5, but his queen took your b pawn back, and his c pawn is still there. [%csl Rb2] }) (16. e4 { Your e pawn hits d5, but that lets his d pawn run forward and hit your knight on c3, on his own terms. [%cal Rd5d4] } 16... d4 17. e5 Qxe5 18. Bxb7 dxc3 19. bxc3 { About level: his d pawn ran forward with a gain of time, and the trades left the material even. [%cal Gb2b3] }) (16. Rac1 { The rook to c1 first. Equal by the numbers: the same plan in another order, with the b pawn still to come. } 16... Rab8 17. b3 c4 { Better for you: pawn to b3 still came, and his c pawn ran forward to c4 all the same. [%cal Gb2b3] }) (16. h4 { The h pawn marches to h4. Equal by the numbers, but it leaves his pair untouched. } 16... Rab8 17. Rac1 h6 18. b3 { Slightly better for you: your h pawn gained a little space, but pawn to b3 still had to come, and the pair stands. [%cal Gb2b3] }) (16. Rab1 { The rook goes to b1, behind the b pawn. Close in the numbers, but the b pawn stays at home, and his pair is left alone. } 16... Rab8 17. Rbc1 h6 18. e3 { About level: your rook went to b1 and then to c1, and his c and d pawns still stand side by side. [%cal Gb2b3] }) (16. e3 { The e pawn steps up to e3. Solid, and close in the numbers, but it does nothing to his pair. } 16... Rab8 17. b3 h6 { Slightly better for you, but pawn to b3 came a move late, and his c and d pawns still stand side by side. [%cal Gb2b3] }) 16... c4 { Aaron Nimzowitsch, who wrote the pawn-play book My System, runs his c pawn on anyway: the pair no longer stands side by side. Take it with the b pawn! [%cal Yb3c4] } 17. bxc4 { Your b pawn takes the pawn that ran forward, and his d5 pawn is left with no pawn of its own beside it. [%csl Rd5] } (17. b4 { The b pawn steps up beside his pawn, to b4. A pawn of the pair still falls later, but his c pawn becomes a passed pawn. } 17... d4 18. Nh5 Qe5 19. Qxd4 Qxd4 20. Rxd4 Bxg2 21. Kxg2 { Better for you: his d pawn fell, but his c pawn is passed now, and your knight on c3 has to stand in front of it. [%csl Rc4] }) (17. h4 { The h pawn steps up to h4. Close in the numbers, but the pawn on c4 is left standing. } 17... Rab8 18. Rac1 Bc6 19. b4 { Slightly better for you, but the pawn on c4 was never taken, and the d5 pawn still guards it. [%cal Gb3c4] }) (17. Rac1 { The rook comes to c1 and leaves his c pawn standing. Equal by the numbers, but it does not break the pair. } 17... Rab8 18. b4 Qd6 { Better for you, but his c pawn stands on c4, guarded by the d5 pawn, and no pawn of his fell. [%cal Gb3c4] }) 17... Nxc4 { His knight takes back and hits your queen. The pair is broken: d5 is an isolani now, so treat it like one. Queen to d4! [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc4d2,Yd2d4] } 18. Qd4 { Out of the attack, your queen hits the knight on c4 and offers the trade: if he takes, your rook takes back on d4, right in front of the isolani. [%cal Gd4c4,Gd1d4] } (18. Qc2 { The queen steps aside to c2. Safe, but the queens stay on, and your rook never reaches d4. } 18... Nb6 19. Rac1 Rfd8 { Slightly better for you, but his knight is back on b6 guarding d5, and the isolani stands. [%cal Gd2d4,Rb6d5] }) (18. Qe1 { The queen goes back to e1, out of the knight's reach. The queens stay on, and your rook stays on d1. } 18... Nb6 19. Rab1 Rab8 { Slightly better for you, but his knight came back to guard the isolani, and no rook of yours stands in front of it. [%cal Gd2d4] }) (18. Qd3 { The queen steps to d3, where his knight can fork her and your rook on d1. [%cal Rc4b2] } 18... Nb2 19. Qd2 Nxd1 20. Rxd1 { Better for him: his knight forked your queen and rook from b2, and you lost the exchange. [%cal Gd2d4] }) 18... Qxd4 { He takes your queen. Take back with the rook. } 19. Rxd4 { Your rook stands in front of the isolani, the first step against it, and hits the knight on c4 as well. [%csl Rd5][%cal Gd4c4] } 19... Nb6 { His knight runs back to guard the isolani. Now the second step: take it, with the knight from c3! [%cal Yc3d5] } 20. Ncxd5 { The knight takes the isolani. Whatever he takes back with, you take again: your other knight, the bishop and the rook all aim at d5. [%cal Gf4d5,Gg2d5,Gd4d5] } (20. Nfxd5 { The other knight takes d5. Equal by the numbers: the same gain, just the other knight first. } 20... Nexd5 21. Nxd5 Bxd5 22. Bxd5 Nxd5 23. Rxd5 { Better for you: the trades end in the very same rook ending, a pawn up. [%cal Gc3d5] }) (20. a4 { The a pawn goes to a4. A little weaker, and slow: it leaves d5 standing for another move. } 20... Rac8 21. Nb5 Rc4 22. Rxc4 Nxc4 { Better for you, but a pair of rooks came off, and the isolani on d5 still stands. [%cal Gc3d5] }) (20. Rad1 { The other rook joins first. Slower: d5 could be taken right now, and this gives him a free move. } 20... Rfe8 21. e3 Nc6 22. R4d2 d4 23. exd4 { Better for you: your e pawn took the d pawn that ran forward, so you are a pawn up after all, only slower. [%cal Gc3d5] }) (20. e3 { The e pawn goes to e3 first. A little weaker: d5 could be taken right now, and this puts it off. } 20... Rfd8 21. Rad1 Nc6 22. R4d2 d4 23. exd4 { Slightly better for you: his d pawn ran on to d4 and your e pawn took it, a pawn of the pair won three moves late. [%cal Gc3d5] }) 20... Bxd5 { His bishop takes back. Take again with the knight from f4, and keep your bishop and rook behind it. [%cal Yf4d5] } 21. Nxd5 { The knight takes back, and your bishop and rook still stand behind it. [%cal Gg2d5,Gd4d5] } (21. Rxd5 { The rook takes on d5, but a knight takes it straight back: a rook for a bishop. } 21... Nbxd5 22. Nxd5 { Better for him: you gave a rook for his bishop, and one pawn does not pay for it. [%cal Gf4d5] }) (21. Bxd5 { The bishop takes back instead. It keeps the pawn too, but after the trades a knight of his stays on the board. } 21... Nexd5 22. Nxd5 { Better for you, a pawn up, but knights stay on, instead of the clean rook ending you get by taking with the knight. [%cal Gf4d5] }) 21... Nexd5 { His knight takes back. Now the bishop, not the rook: his other knight still guards d5. [%cal Yg2d5] } 22. Bxd5 { The bishop takes back, and your rook stands ready to take last. Watch. [%tail] } (22. Rxd5 { The rook takes on d5, and his knight on b6 takes it back: the exchange given up. } 22... Nxd5 23. Bxd5 { Slightly better for him: your bishop got the knight back, but his rook against your bishop is worth more than your extra pawn. [%cal Gg2d5] }) 22... Nxd5 23. Rxd5 { A rook ending, and you are a pawn up. Hanging pawns: one ran forward, the other was left as an isolani on d5, and the isolani fell. [%csl Gd5] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: hanging pawns, Spa 1926"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/h7Lm2IA0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Tartakower - Saemisch, Spa 1926 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "r1qr2k1/p3bb1p/1pp1pp2/8/2PP4/1Q1N2B1/P4PPP/3R1RK1 w - - 0 21"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1qr2k1/p3bb1p/1pp1pp2/8/2PP4/1Q1N2B1/P4PPP/3R1RK1_w_-_-_0_21"]

{ You have Black, as Fritz Saemisch did, whose name is on the Saemisch lines of two famous openings. His c and d pawns are hanging pawns: side by side, with no pawn of his on the columns either side. }
21. Nc1 { His knight goes back to c1, a mistake: on f4 it would have been far more active. What now? } 21... c5 { Your c pawn hits d4, and the pair has no easy answer: if the pawn stays there, you take it. [%cal Gc5d4] } (21... e5 { The tension released: your e pawn hits d4 from the other side, but he can simply take it. } 22. dxe5 Qg4 23. h3 Qxc4 24. Qe3 { Level: his pair is gone, but it cost you your e pawn, and his pawn now stands on e5. [%cal Gc6c5] }) (21... b5 { Your b pawn hits c4 instead of d4, and he simply takes it. } 22. cxb5 c5 23. Ne2 cxd4 24. Rc1 { Level: you won his d pawn, but it cost your b pawn, and his pawn stands on b5 now. [%cal Gc6c5] }) (21... Rd7 { The rook steps up to d7, to double on the d column, but it is slow: he has time to bring help. } 22. Ne2 Qe8 23. Qf3 Rad8 { Level: his knight came back to e2 to guard d4, and his pair still stands side by side. [%cal Gc6c5,Re2d4] }) (21... Rxd4 { Your rook grabs the pawn, but his rook takes it back. } 22. Rxd4 e5 23. Rdd1 { Lost for you: your rook went for one pawn, as his rook on d1 guarded d4. [%cal Gc6c5] }) (21... h5 { The h pawn goes forward on the king side, far from his pair. } 22. Ne2 e5 23. h3 Qf5 { Level: you spent the move on your h pawn, his knight came back to e2, and both pawns of his pair are still there. [%cal Gc6c5] }) (21... Qa6 { The queen hits c4 from a6, but his queen guards it, and the pawn can step on out of your queen's reach. } 22. Bh4 Bh5 23. c5 { Level: his c pawn stepped forward to c5 on its own terms, and no pawn of his pair fell. [%cal Gc6c5] }) (21... Bh5 { Your bishop hits his rook on d1, but that is only a threat, and his pair is left alone. } 22. Rd2 e5 23. c5+ Bf7 { Level: his c pawn stepped to c5 and opened his queen's diagonal with check, and your bishop had to run back to f7 to block it. [%cal Rb3g8] }) 22. d5 { His d pawn runs forward to d5 rather than be taken. } 22... exd5 { Your e pawn takes the pawn that ran forward, and his c pawn has to take back or stay a pawn down. [%cal Ge6d5] } (22... Qb7 { Your queen steps to b7 and leaves his d pawn standing. It can run on. } 23. d6 Bf8 { Level: his d pawn got to d6, deep in your half, and your bishop had to step back to f8. [%csl Rd6] }) (22... Qd7 { Your queen steps to d7, but his d pawn can still run on and hit your bishop. } 23. d6 Bf8 { About level: his d pawn now stands on d6, guarded by his rook, and you never won it. [%cal Rd1d6][%csl Rd6] }) (22... Bf8 { The bishop drops back to f8 first, and the pawn on d5 is not taken. } 23. Bh4 Bg7 24. Qg3 Kf8 { Slightly better for him: his pawn still stands on d5, and his queen and bishop gather against your king. [%cal Ge6d5] }) (22... b5 { Your b pawn hits c4, the other pawn, but his d pawn can run on and hit your bishop. } 23. d6 Bf8 24. Qf3 Rd7 { Much better for him: while your b pawn poked at c4, his d pawn reached d6, and your pieces are cramped behind it. [%csl Rd6] }) (22... Rd7 { The rook steps up to d7 before taking, and that gives his knight time to come back. } 23. Ne2 exd5 24. cxd5 Qd8 { Better for him: you took on d5 a move late, and his new pawn there holds, guarded by his rook and queen. [%cal Rd1d5,Rb3d5][%csl Rd5] }) 23. cxd5 { His c pawn takes back: the last pawn of his pair now stands alone on d5. } 23... Qb7 { Your queen steps onto the diagonal to d5. Now if his queen ever takes on d5 with check, yours takes her back. [%cal Gb7d5] } (23... Bxd5 { The tempting capture: your bishop takes d5 at once. But with the bishop gone from f7, the diagonal to your king is open behind it. [%cal Rb3g8] } 24. Rxd5 Rxd5 25. Qxd5+ { Lost for you: his queen took last on d5, with check, and you are a bishop down for one pawn. [%cal Gc8b7,Rd5g8] }) (23... Rxd5 { The rook grabs the pawn at once, but his rook takes it, and your bishop cannot take back without opening the diagonal to your king. [%cal Rb3g8] } 24. Rxd5 Qc6 25. Rfd1 { Lost for you: your rook went for one pawn, since your bishop could not take back on d5 without his queen taking it with check. [%cal Gc8b7] }) (23... Qf5 { Your queen steps to f5 to hit d5, but his queen gets a free move, and his d pawn is not taken yet. } 24. Qe3 Bf8 25. d6 { About level: his d pawn ran on to d6, and the pawn you were after got away. [%cal Gc8b7] }) (23... c4 { Your c pawn hits his queen, but she has a good square to go to. } 24. Qe3 Qc5 25. d6 Qxe3 26. fxe3 { Level: after the queens came off, his pawn stands on d6, and d5 was never won. [%cal Gc8b7] }) (23... Re8 { The rook takes the e column, and the pawn on d5 is left alone for now. } 24. Qf3 Qb7 25. Ne2 Bf8 { Better for him: your rook on e8 found nothing to hit, and his knight came back while d5 stayed his. [%cal Gc8b7] }) (23... Rd7 { The rook steps up to d7, a slow move that gives his knight time. } 24. Ne2 Bf8 25. Rfe1 Qd8 { Better for him: while your rook and queen lined up on the d column, his knight got back into play and his rook took the e column. [%csl Rd5] }) 24. Rfe1 { His rook takes the e column and faces your bishop on e7. [%cal Re1e7] } 24... Bxd5 { Hanging pawns: both pawns of his pair are gone, for your e pawn. His c pawn took on d5 and fell there to your bishop. [%csl Gd5] } (24... Rxd5 { The rook takes first, but then the trades on d5 and e7 end with his queen taking last. } 25. Rxd5 Bxd5 26. Rxe7 Qxe7 27. Qxd5+ { Level: after the trades his queen took last on d5, with check, and the material is even again. [%cal Gf7d5] }) (24... Qxd5 { The queen takes d5, but his rook on d1 simply takes her. } 25. Rxd5 Bxd5 { Lost for you: your queen went for a rook and a pawn. [%cal Gf7d5] }) (24... Bf8 { The bishop steps back to f8 first. The d5 pawn can wait a move, but that gives his bishop a free move. } 25. Bh4 Bxd5 26. Qc3 Bg7 { Slightly better for you: you won d5 a move late, but his bishop and queen now hit f6 twice, and only your bishop guards it. [%cal Rh4f6,Rc3f6][%csl Gd5] }) (24... Rd7 { The rook steps to d7, to take d5 with the rook later. That gives his queen a free move. } 25. Qc3 Rxd5 26. Rxd5 Bxd5 { About level: the pawn fell after all, but his queen got a free move to c3, where it hits your c pawn. [%cal Gf7d5] }) (24... Kg7 { Your king steps off his queen's diagonal first. Safe, but it gives his bishop a free move. [%cal Rb3g8] } 25. Bh4 Bxd5 26. Qg3+ Kf8 { Level: you won d5 after all, but his bishop and queen came at your king first. [%cal Gf7d5] }) (24... Rac8 { Your last rook comes to the c column, and d5 is left standing for a move. } 25. Bh4 Bxd5 26. Qg3+ Kh8 { Level: d5 fell after all, but his bishop got to h4 first, and after his queen's check your king went to the corner. [%csl Gd5] }) (24... Re8 { Your rook meets his on the e column instead of taking the pawn. } 25. Ne2 Bf8 26. Qf3 Bg7 { Better for him: you spent the time on the e column, his knight came back, and d5 stayed his. [%cal Gf7d5] }) 0-1


