{ Margate, 1939: you have White as Jose Raul Capablanca, world champion from 1921 to 1927. The minority attack: two pawns attack three. Your b pawn runs at his c pawn, and after the trades he is left with a weak pawn. Push it to b5. [%cal Yb4b5][%csl Rc6] }
23. b5 { The attack starts: your b pawn hits his c pawn, and your queen and rook stand behind it. [%cal Gb5c6,Gd3b5] } (23. Nc5 { The knight jumps to a fine square, but it does nothing to his pawns. } 23... b6 24. Na6 Rac8 { About level: his b pawn chased your knight to the edge, and all his pawns stand healthy. [%cal Gb4b5] }) (23. Qc2 { The queen steps back to prepare the push. It is slow, and he uses the time. } 23... a6 24. Nc5 Qc7 { Level: while your queen stepped back, his a pawn came to a6 to guard b5, and your b pawn has to wait. [%cal Gb4b5] }) (23. h4 { A king-side push that does nothing for your plan, and the pawn on h4 has no guard. } 23... Re4 24. Nc3 Rxh4 { Better for him: his rook took your h pawn, while your b pawn never moved. [%cal Gb4b5] }) (23. e4 { The e pawn breaks in the centre, the other plan in this pawn structure. Here it only opens lines for his pieces. } 23... dxe4 24. Qd2 { Much better for him: his pawn took on e4 and chased your queen, and your d pawn now stands alone in the centre. [%cal Gb4b5][%csl Rd4] }) 23... cxb5 { He takes, and his d pawn is now an isolani: no pawn of his stands on the c or e column to guard it. Take back with the queen. [%cal Yd3b5][%csl Rd5] } 24. Qxb5 { Your queen hits two of his pawns at once, on b7 and d5. [%cal Gb5b7,Gb5d5] } (24. Rxb5 { The rook takes back instead, and your other rook on c1 loses its guard. [%csl Rc1] } 24... Rac8 25. Rd1 b6 { Slightly better for you, no more: his rook took the c column, and your queen never reached b5. [%csl Rc8] }) (24. Nc5 { The knight jumps in before you take the pawn back. } 24... b6 25. Na6 Rec8 26. Qxb5 { About level: your queen got the pawn back only after his b pawn had chased your knight to a6. [%cal Gd3b5] }) 24... Re7 { His rook guards b7 along the seventh row. Now your rook: to c5! [%cal Re7b7,Yc1c5] } 25. Rc5 { Your rook and queen now stand in one line on the fifth row, both aimed at his pawn on d5. [%cal Gb5d5] } (25. Nc5 { Just as good: the knight comes to c5 instead, but it stands in your rook's way. } 25... Rb8 { Better for you, but his pawn on d5 still stands, and your rook can no longer reach c5. [%csl Rd5] }) (25. Rc2 { Just as good: the rook steps to c2 on the open c column, but it does not touch his d pawn. } 25... Ne6 { Better for you, but his knight came to e6 and now covers c5, the square your rook wanted. [%cal Gc2c5][%csl Rc5] }) (25. Rc3 { A little slower: the rook lifts to c3, but it stops short of c5. } 25... Ne6 { Slightly better for you: before your rook could reach c5, his knight came to e6 to cover it. [%cal Re6c5,Gc3c5] }) (25. Nb2 { About as good: the knight turns back on its way to d3, and it leaves his d pawn alone. } 25... Ne8 { Slightly better for you: your knight is still on its way, and his pawn on d5 lives on. [%cal Gc1c5] }) (25. Qc5 { Close in the numbers: your queen steps to c5 and offers a trade of queens. } 25... Qd8 { Slightly better for you: his queen stepped back out of the trade, and his d pawn still stands. [%csl Rd5] }) (25. Qb3 { A little weaker: your queen drops back to b3, where she still eyes d5 from afar. [%cal Gb3d5] } 25... Ne6 { Slightly better for you: his knight on e6 now guards c5, the square your rook would use against his d pawn. [%cal Gc1c5] }) 25... b6 { His pawn on b6 kicks your rook. Take on d5! [%cal Yc5d5] } 26. Rxd5 { That is the minority attack: your b pawn traded itself for his c pawn, his d pawn was left alone, and your rook has taken it. You are a pawn up. [%csl Gd5] } (26. Rc6 { Close in the numbers: the rook hits his queen, but it leaves the pawn on d5 standing. } 26... Qd8 { Slightly better for you, but his queen stepped back to d8 and still guards the pawn on d5. [%cal Gc5d5] }) (26. Qc6 { Your queen offers a trade instead of taking the pawn. } 26... Qxc6 27. Rxc6 { Slightly better for you: the queens are gone, and his d pawn still stands. [%cal Gc5d5] }) 1-0


