{ You have White, at Baden-Baden in 1925. Your knight on e4 has just taken one of his knights. }
13... dxe4 { He takes back with his d pawn, a slip: his knight should have taken. Now his bishop hits your pawn on b4. What now? [%cal Rd6b4] } 14. b5 { Your b pawn steps out of the bishop's reach and hits his c pawn at once. } (14. a3 { The a pawn guards b4, as Nimzowitsch, author of the strategy book My System, played here. Safe, but slow. [%cal Ga3b4] } 14... O-O 15. g4 Bd7 { About level: your b pawn is safe but hits nothing, and his pawns on the queen's side stay healthy. [%cal Gb4b5] }) (14. Nxe6 { Your knight takes his bishop first. } 14... Qxe6 15. b5 O-O 16. bxc6 bxc6 { Slightly better for you: his c pawn is weak, but your knight is gone, and his queen now eyes your pawn on a2. [%cal Re6a2,Gb4b5] }) (14. Qc2 { The queen comes to c2 and hits his pawn on e4: if his bishop grabs your b pawn, a couple of trades win that pawn back. [%cal Gc2e4] } 14... Bd7 15. b5 c5 { About level: his bishop came to d7 in time, and his c pawn stepped on to c5, where no pawn of yours can take it. [%cal Gb4b5] }) (14. Qa4 { Your queen guards the pawn on b4. [%cal Ga4b4] } 14... Bd7 15. b5 c5 { Level: his c pawn ran on to c5 instead of waiting to be taken, and none of his pawns is weak. [%cal Gb4b5] }) (14. Rc1 { The rook comes to the c column first, and your b pawn is left unguarded. } 14... Bxb4 15. Nxe6 Qxe6 16. Rc4 a5 { Level, though you are a pawn down: while your rook went to the c column, his bishop took your b pawn. [%csl Rb4] }) (14. d3 { The d pawn hits his e pawn, but it forgets your pawn on b4. } 14... exd3 15. Bxd3 Bxb4 { Level, but a pawn down: the centre opened, and his bishop took your b pawn. [%csl Rb4] }) (14. Kh1 { The king steps into the corner, and your b pawn waits. } 14... Bd7 15. a3 h5 { Level: while your king stepped aside, his bishop came to d7 to guard c6, and now his h pawn marches. [%cal Gb4b5,Rd7c6] }) (14. g4 { Your g pawn goes forward on the king's side, and the queen's side is left alone. } 14... Bd7 15. a3 a5 { About level: his bishop reached d7, and his a pawn now hits your b pawn. [%cal Gb4b5,Ra5b4] }) (14. a4 { The a pawn steps up to support the push, but your pawn on b4 is still unguarded. } 14... Bxb4 15. a5 Bd7 { About level, but a pawn down: his bishop took your b pawn, and his c pawn is safe. [%csl Rb4] }) 14... O-O { He castles his king to safety. What now? } 15. bxc6 { Your pawn takes on c6 and now hits b7, so his b pawn has to take back. [%cal Gc6b7] } (15. Rc1 { The rook joins the c column, and he gets the time to take on b5 himself. } 15... cxb5 16. Bxb5 Rac8 { Level: his c pawn traded itself for your b pawn, and his pawns on a7 and b7 stand healthy. [%cal Gb5c6] }) (15. Rb1 { The rook comes to the b column, but your bishop stands in its way, and he takes on b5 first. } 15... cxb5 16. Nxe6 Qxe6 17. Bxb5 { Level: he took on b5 before you could take on c6, and nothing of his is left to attack. }) (15. a4 { The a pawn comes up to support your b pawn. } 15... Bd7 { About level: your pawn got its support, but his bishop on d7 now guards c6, and he can wait. [%cal Gb5c6,Rd7c6] }) (15. d3 { The d pawn hits his e pawn in the centre instead. } 15... Nd5 16. Nxe6 fxe6 { Level: your d pawn left e3 unguarded, and his knight settled on d5, where it hits that pawn. [%cal Gb5c6] }) (15. g4 { A king-side push that hands him a free move. } 15... Bd7 16. bxc6 bxc6 { About level: you took on c6 in the end, but his bishop on d7 guards the pawn now. [%cal Gb5c6,Rd7c6] }) (15. b6 { The pawn runs on past his c pawn. } 15... axb6 { Slightly better for him: your pawn was lost for nothing, and he is simply a pawn up. }) (15. Nxe6 { Your knight takes his bishop before the pawns trade. } 15... Qxe6 16. bxc6 bxc6 { Slightly better for you: his lone c pawn is weak, but the knight that would hit it from d4 is gone, and his queen eyes a2. [%cal Re6a2,Gb5c6] }) (15. Nxc6 { The knight takes on c6 at once. } 15... bxc6 16. bxc6 { Much better for him: you gave a whole knight for two pawns. [%cal Gb5c6] }) 15... bxc6 { He takes back. What now? } 16. Rc1 { Your rook comes to the half-open c column, straight at his lone pawn on c6. [%cal Gc1c6] } (16. Nxc6 { The knight grabs the lone pawn at once. } 16... Qb7 17. Bxf6 Qxc6 18. Rc1 Qd7 19. Bc3 Bxa2 { Slightly better for you, but your extra pawn is gone again: his queen hit your knight and bishop at once, and his bishop took a2. [%csl Ra2] }) (16. Qa4 { Close in the numbers: your queen goes after the lone pawn from a4. } 16... Bd7 17. Nxc6 Bxc6 18. Qxc6 { Slightly better for you: the pawn fell, but your queen now stands alone on c6, where his rooks can chase her. [%cal Ga1c1] }) (16. Qc2 { Just as good in the numbers: your queen hits both his lone c pawn and his e pawn from c2. [%cal Gc2c6,Gc2e4] } 16... Bd7 { Slightly better for you: his bishop came back to d7 to guard the pawn, and your rook is still at home. [%cal Ga1c1,Rd7c6] }) (16. Nxe6 { Your knight takes his bishop now. } 16... Qxe6 { Slightly better for you: the trade cost you the knight that pressed his c pawn, and his queen on e6 now looks at your a2 pawn. [%cal Ga1c1,Re6a2] }) (16. Rb1 { The rook leaves a1 for the b column, and your a2 pawn loses its guard. } 16... Bxa2 17. Ra1 Bd5 { About level, but a pawn down: his bishop took your a2 pawn as soon as your rook left it. [%cal Ga1c1] }) (16. d3 { Your d pawn strikes at his e pawn, and his c pawn gets a rest. } 16... Bd7 17. dxe4 Qxe4 { Level: his bishop guarded c6 in time, and his queen took back on e4 in the centre. [%cal Ga1c1,Rd7c6] }) 16... Rfc8 { His rook comes over to guard the pawn. What now? } 17. Ba6 { Your bishop hits his rook on c8 and covers b7, so his queen cannot use that square. [%cal Ga6c8,Ga6b7] } (17. Nxe6 { Just as good in the numbers: your knight takes his bishop instead. } 17... Qxe6 { Better for you, but the knight that hit his c pawn is gone, and the pawn still stands. [%cal Ge2a6] }) (17. Nxc6 { The knight takes the pawn at once. } 17... Qb7 18. Bxf6 Rxc6 19. Bc3 { Slightly better for you: you are a pawn up, but his queen on b7 hit two pieces, and the knights came off. [%cal Ge2a6] }) (17. Rxc6 { The rook takes the pawn at once. } 17... Rxc6 18. Nxc6 Qb7 19. Bxf6 Qxc6 20. Bc3 { Slightly better for you: the pawn is yours, but a pair of rooks and both knights came off, and his queen sits on c6 in its place. [%cal Ge2a6] }) (17. Rc2 { The rook steps up to c2, ready to double on the c column. } 17... Bd7 { Slightly better for you: his bishop came to d7 to guard the pawn, and your bishop never reached a6. [%cal Ge2a6,Rd7c6] }) 17... Rc7 { His rook steps out of reach. What now? } 18. Rxc6 { Your rook takes his lone pawn, and your knight on d4 guards the rook. Now watch. [%tail][%cal Gd4c6] } (18. Nxc6 { Just as good: the knight takes the lone pawn instead of the rook. } 18... Qd7 19. Ne5 Bxe5 20. Bxe5 { Better for you: the pawn is yours this way too, and the knight went on to trade itself for a bishop. [%cal Gc1c6] }) (18. Nxe6 { Your knight takes his bishop before the pawn. } 18... Qxe6 { Slightly better for you: your knight is traded for his bishop, and the pawn on c6 still stands. [%cal Gc1c6] }) (18. Qa4 { Your queen joins the attack on the pawn from a4. } 18... Rb8 19. Nxe6 Qxe6 { Slightly better for you: his rook turned on your bishop, the knight traded itself for his bishop, and his c pawn still stands. [%cal Gc1c6] }) 18... Bd7 19. Rxc7 Bxc7 20. h3 { The minority attack: two pawns against three left him a lone pawn on c6, and your rook took it. You are a pawn up. [%csl Gc6] } 1-0
